cff-version: 1.2.0
title: "Value-Field Theory (VFT)"
message: "If you use this theory or repository, please cite this repository."
authors:
  - family-names: "Nuno"
    given-names: "T."
    affiliation: "Independent Researcher"
abstract: >
  Value-Field Theory (VFT) is a structural framework and common state
  representation for describing how a shared multidimensional real-resource
  world K, actor-specific access and usability views K_i, and actor-specific
  subjective evaluation and expectation states P_i condition actor-side
  processes and events A_i. The Core is not a standalone predictive hypothesis;
  empirical restrictions are supplied by domain-specific projections and
  measurement models. K_i is not required to be a set-theoretic subset of K;
  it is a derived actor-relative access, usability, exercisability, or
  referenceability view of the shared resource world. For another actor a,
  P_j(a) denotes actor j's evaluation, belief, credit assessment, or expectation
  about a, rather than a state held by a. Delta K(S,tau) denotes an S-indexed
  interval change descriptor. In the general path-aware form,
  Z_S(tau)=M_S((K_t)_{t in tau},(A_t)_{t in tau}) and
  Delta K(S,tau)=delta_S(Z_S(tau)) in D_S. Endpoint-only changes are a special
  case using Y_S(t)=M_S(K_t) and delta_S(Y_S(t0),Y_S(t1)); arithmetic differences
  are a further special case of additive quantitative projections. Delta P_i is
  likewise a change descriptor or state transition and need not be arithmetic.
  The same S-indexed interval-change schema can be used across micro and macro
  scales even when value domains, coordinates, units, and aggregation rules
  differ. Actor states and interval processes are temporally typed so that
  (K_t0,P_t0) condition A_(t0,t1], which may contribute to (K_t1,P_t1), without
  imposing a universal transition function. E_i[Delta K(S,tau)] denotes an
  actor-specific expected realized resource change where the relevant Delta K
  is represented on a quantitative projection for which an expectation is
  defined. Planned or chosen resource changes are distinct from such forecasts
  and may be represented by projection-local variables such as x_i. VFT does
  not fix a universal probability measure, expectation-formation equation,
  action-selection rule, market-equilibrium condition, or aggregation law.
  Economic equilibrium, budget constraints, accounting identities, surplus,
  decentralized adjustment, micro-macro linkage, and reputation- or
  credit-economy models are treated as domain-specific projection candidates
  requiring additional economic and measurement assumptions.
repository-code: "https://github.com/nuno929/vf-model"
